Subject: [PATCH] ntfs: don't let a fatal signal interrupt metadata updates

read_mapping_folio() waits for the read it submits with
folio_wait_locked_killable(), so it returns -EINTR as soon as the caller
has a fatal signal pending. On the paths that change metadata that error
stops an update half way: a SIGKILL or SIGTERM that arrives while a
thread is in unlink(), creating a file or allocating clusters leaves
clusters allocated in $Bitmap, an MFT record in use, or a directory index
inconsistent, and errors=remount-ro turns the volume read-only.

Add ntfs_read_metadata_folio(). On -EINTR the read is already in flight,
so it looks the folio up again and waits for the read uninterruptibly,
returning it when it is uptodate and -EIO otherwise. Use it for every
metadata read on an update path: $Bitmap and $MFT/$BITMAP updates, the
cluster allocator's $Bitmap scan, MFT record mapping, allocation and
$MFTMirr updates, attribute reads and writes (which carry directory index
blocks), and ntfs_attr_set(). Lookups and mount-time reads are unchanged.

Not upstream: local patch against v7.3.1, also submitted for ntfs-next
(github.com/xkeyC/linux-ntfs branch fix-eintr-metadata-update). Testing
is described in README.md.
---
